joining us now from perthshire is  scotland's deputy first minister john  swinney thank you very much for being  on the programme uh today um starting  off on covid and nicola sturgeon's got  much better approval ratings than boris  johnson  for the way that she's dealt with the  covert crisis but actually if you look  at the recent data from the office of  national statistics  national records of scotland  proportionately more people have been  dying with covert in scotland than  england so  is nicola sturgeon let off the hook i  think if you look back over the whole  of the pandemic what you see is that the  number of cases in scotland has been  about 30 percent less than it's been in  england and in wales so i think we've  seen a comparatively  lower level of the prevalence of the  virus and obviously we've taken  different measures to try to tackle the  situation  within scotland but i think when we're  dealing with  a pandemic dealing with the fact that  people's lives are affected and some  lives  tragically are lost i think it's  important to focus on the fact that  we're doing everything we possibly can  do  in the best possible way to try to  protect the population and that's been  the approach that we've taken as a  government  i'm a bit confused at the first part of  your answer there saying that there's  been fewer cases in scotland and  it's not a great thing is it if you've  got fewer cases but more people are  dying  well obviously the effective covert will  will  affect the the underlying health of  people within a particular  in society are unhealthier than people  in england well obviously there are  challenges that  people will have with underlying health  conditions that will vary from  individual to individual around the  society but in general  the case the cases that have presented  themselves in scotland  have been about 30 lower than in the  rest of the united kingdom  and what we're trying to do in all that  we do on a weekly basis  is to minimize the prevalence of the  virus to reduce the cases and we're  seeing  steadily a reduction in the number of  cases where we're now  seeing cases per 100 000 in scotland  being  around about 120 and that's come down  quite significantly in the course of the  last few weeks we've been taking  measures  to reduce the prevalence of the virus  there's been an awful lot of  conversations and debate about what  happens at christmas but of course new  year or hogmanay in scotland is a really  big celebration for  many families as well are you worried  that people might ignore the rules and  get together to celebrate it's been a  really difficult one for us i have to  say  sophie it's i think all of us are very  nervous  about the christmas period we obviously  understand that everybody will be  feeling a sense of  desperation almost to be with our  families and  to to have more social interaction with  those that they love  and obviously in scotland we have the  added dimension that there's a lot of  socializing around about  hagmoney but what we've decided to do is  to agree a limited amount of extra  social interaction around about  christmas  and unfortunately say to people for hug  money we're just gonna have to  stick at home and not have that social  interaction  and that'll be difficult this year but  what i would appeal to people to do is  to to recognize that  it's this year we're seeing um improved  prospects of tackling the virus with the  reductions in caseload or the the  arrival of the vaccine that gives us  some hope that  maybe by next hug money we can enjoy it  as we normally do  fingers crossed for next year certainly  we hope that by then we'll be able to  have a bit of a return to normality now  the smp's westminster leader in  blackfood has apologized after tweeting  a photographer called orly taylor who  posted a picture from the highlands i'm  sure you've seen the coverage he tweeted  saying as you live in the south of  england  and travel to scotland there's only for  permitted reasons i'm sure there will be  a valid reason as to why you're posting  a photo from the north of scotland last  night now  of course it then transpired that ollie  taylor actually lives about five minutes  away from where he took the photo  isn't that an example of you know the  worst kind of covered snooping  think it was a mistake by ian and he  acknowledged that and he apologized for  it  and i think what it it demonstrates is  that there's  a lot of anxiety about and where there  can be movement around  society which can lead to the spread of  the virus and  i suppose all i would say is that it's  really important that all of us  minimize our social interaction and  minimize our traveling around the  country because that's what the virus is  searching out for it searching out for  people moving from  areas of high prevalence to low  prevalence and the spread of the virus  and  we we've gone for a tiered system in  scotland for some weeks now where  the highlands is in a much lower set of  restrictions  than in the central belt or in very  large parts of  of england as well so what we're trying  to do is to make sure that people where  there is a low prevalence  are able to experience as much of life  as they possibly can do  and to keep virus levels at a low level  now that's a challenge  where you have some degree of social  interaction and moving around even in  areas like the highlands  and so i think if people follow the  rules then it makes it a lot  safer and a lot more reassuring for  everybody else  although of course important to say that  ollie taylor it seems was  following the rules um well absolutely  i'm sorry please please  don't don't i i completely accept what  you're putting to me and i wouldn't want  to suggest otherwise  but just as a general observation the  more we minimize our travel  the better it is for minimizing the  spread of the virus yeah understood  understood  um i'm keen to talk about uh alex  salmond the the scottish parliament has  voted twice  to force the scottish government to  release the legal advice that it  received in relation to that trial  you've refused twice why are you  ignoring the will of the scottish  parliament  well i've not refused twice what i've  said what the  the government's original position was  that we do not  to release legal advice and no  government releases  legal advice except in in very  exceptional circumstances now what i've  said to parliament  is that we will consider the fact that  parliament has voted in that way  that consideration is ongoing at the  present moment and that work is being  undertaken to determine  whether what approach we should take to  responding to the votes that parliament  has undertaken so  the issue's not closed it's very much  under active consideration within the  scottish government  and i'll update parliament as soon as  i've got to a conclusion on the handling  of that issue  so to be clear you might you may very  well publish that advice  well there's a possibility that we might  do that because parliament has asked us  to do that and i'm now reflecting on  that particular issue so  at no stage have i closed the door on  this issue i've simply set out the fact  that the government  ordinarily and that when we're not  unusual in this respect governments  don't publish their legal advice and  indeed  the ministerial code under which i  operate indicates that i'm not allowed  under the ministerial code  other than in exceptional circumstances  to release legal advice and i need the  prior consent  of our law officers before i do that so  the rules are quite clear that i  shouldn't be releasing the legal advice  but parliament has obviously voted for  that to be the case and i've said to  parliament  i'm considering that issue so i've not  closed the door and it's under very  active consideration  um active consideration when are you  likely to make a decision  well we'll have to move out quite soon  on this because obviously i understand  the committee has  um it's got its own work program to take  forward and we've cooperated fully with  the committee we've sent  thousands of pages of documents to the  committee that are looking at this issue  and i want to continue that cooperation  so i'm mindful of the fact that the  committee  wants to have that information to enable  it to complete its work and  and we're working as swiftly as we can  to address those issues  okay thank you very much for coming on  the show and sending out and some of  that much appreciated good  talk

